'''Thaiphum''' (Thaiphum Chin) is a Southern [[Kuki-Chin languages|Kuki-Chin language]] spoken in 4 villages of [[Matupi Township|Matupi]] township, [[Chin State]], [[Burma]]. Thaiphum is partially intelligible with neighboring languages, such as the [[Khumi language|Eastern Khumi]] varieties of Khenlak, Asang, and Rengcaa.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |date=2016 |title=Myanmar |url=http://www.ethnologue.com/country/MM/languages |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161010180533/http://www.ethnologue.com/country/MM/languages |archive-date=2016-10-10 |website=Ethnologue: Languages of the World}}</ref> Thaiphum has 72%–75% [[lexical similarity]] with Eastern Khumi, 69%–71% with [[Khumi language|Khumi]], 65%–68% with [[Mro-Khimi language|Mro-Khimi]], and 66%–71% with [[Nga La language|Matu Chin]].<ref name=":0" />

